<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samlaut uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samlaut uprising was a peasant revolt that broke out in 1967 around Samlaut in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battambang">Battambang</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, provoked by forced government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> purchases and land grievances. The army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> suppressed it harshly. It weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a>'s government and is often seen as an early step towards the Cambodian Civil War, as communist cadres drew on rural discontent.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The revolt came in two phases, in 1967 and 1968, and was made up mainly of dissident peasants, with leftist intellectuals among its leaders, against Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a>'s Sangkum regime. Its chief economic cause is often held to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> collection ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> in early 1967, under which the army bought the harvest at low prices, in some cases forcing farmers to surrender it at gunpoint. Lon Nol acted because much of the crop was being sold illegally to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>, which paid more than the government. Peasants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battambang">Battambang</a> also lost land to plantations and were burdened by debt, and the settlement of Khmer Krom families added to the pressure.</p><p>Accounts differ. David Chandler holds that the revolt sprang from local grievances and did not follow orders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Communist Party of Kampuchea</a>, while Ben Kiernan argues that the party was planning an uprising across the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 2 April 1967 peasants in the Samlaut subdistrict attacked soldiers collecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, killed two and took rifles. That afternoon about 200 rebels attacked the government's agricultural settlement at Stung Kranhoung. Sihanouk was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> sent paratroopers and police, who are said to have been offered a bounty for each rebel's head. Within three weeks nearly 200 rebels were captured and 19 killed, but the revolt spread. On 22 April Sihanouk blamed three leftist deputies, Khieu Samphan, Hou Yuon and Hu Nim. Lon Nol resigned at the end of April. In May and June aircraft bombed villages and villages were burned, and in June Sihanouk declared the rebellion over, by which time more than 4,000 villagers had fled into the forests.</p><p>A better organised second phase began on 17 January 1968 with an attack by the Revolutionary Army of Kampuchea at Baydamram. On 25 February attacks followed in several provinces, and more than 10,000 villagers took to the forests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> returned as Minister of Defence in late January. The army destroyed a rebel network, including a rifle factory at Phnom Veay Chap, on 8 April 1968, and peasants began to return to their villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Rebels in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battambang">Battambang</a> moved south-east to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in other provinces, and by mid-1968 a network of bases existed across the country. Kiernan called the revolt the baptism of fire for the small Cambodian revolutionary movement. North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> gave the rebels little help, since it needed Sihanouk's cooperation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> supplied Sihanouk with arms. The harsh repression increased the rural population's resentment, and the movement grew until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a>'s coup of March 1970 overthrew Sihanouk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samlaut Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
